<h2 class="section" id="s33">Acceptance sites</h2>
<div class="para"><p>Version 1 is done when these work:</p>
<a href="/page/34.html" title="Next">More</a></div>
<div class="para"><p>- old.reddit.com: browse, log in, vote, comment
- reddit.com: read
- Wikipedia
- Hacker News
- DuckDuckGo search
- GitHub: read repositories and issues
- typical news and documentation sites, and retro Mac sites</p>
<a href="/page/35.html" title="Next">More</a></div>
<div class="para"><p>Gmail's web application is not a version 1 criterion.</p>
<a href="/page/36.html" title="Next">More</a></div>
<h2 class="section" id="s37">Targets on a 1 GHz G4</h2>
<div class="para"><p>These are to be confirmed by measurement on real hardware.</p>
<a href="/page/38.html" title="Next">More</a></div>
<div class="para"><p>- The application is under 3 MB on disk.
- Aura uses under 16 MB when idle.
- A Wikipedia article is displayed within a few seconds of its bytes
  arriving.
- A laid-out page scrolls smoothly.</p>
<a href="/page/39.html" title="Next">More</a></div>
<h2 class="section" id="s40">Must have</h2>
<h2 class="section" id="s41">Engine</h2>
<div class="para"><p>- HTML: the full parser, the legacy encodings (windows-1252, ISO-8859-1)
  and the `&lt;meta charset&gt;` prescan.
- CSS: cascade, custom properties, media queries.
- Layout: block, inline, inline-block, floats, positioning (relative,
  absolute, fixed, sticky), flexbox, grid, tables, lists, overflow and
  scrolling boxes, `display: contents`, stacking contexts.
- Text: line breaking, white-space modes, alignment, decorations,
  ellipsis, font fallback, Unicode as far as the system fonts cover it.
  Web fonts: TrueType, WOFF, WOFF2.
- Painting: backgrounds, borders, radii, shadows, gradients, opacity, 2D
  transforms, clipping.
- Images: PNG, JPEG, animated GIF, WebP, favicons, SVG as images and
  inline SVG for icons (basic shapes, paths, fills, strokes).
- Forms: text fields, textarea, checkbox, radio, select, buttons, file
  upload, submission (GET, POST, multipart), validation attributes, focus
  and keyboard navigation.
- JavaScript (QuickJS-ng): DOM and events, CSSOM basics (`style`,
  `classList`, `getComputedStyle`, `matchMedia`), timers,
  `requestAnimationFrame`, `fetch` and XHR, URL and URLSearchParams,
  `localStorage` and `sessionStorage`, the history API, MutationObserver,
  IntersectionObserver, ResizeObserver, custom elements and shadow DOM,
  modules and dynamic import, TextEncoder, `document.cookie`.
- Incremental rendering: show the page as it arrives, and lay out again
  only what changed.</p>
<a href="/page/42.html" title="Next">More</a></div>
<h2 class="section" id="s43">Network</h2>
<div class="para"><p>- Open Transport, DNS, HTTP/1.1 with keep-alive, redirects, chunked
  encoding, gzip, deflate and brotli.
- HTTP and HTTPS proxy; basic and digest authentication.
- TLS 1.2 at least, certificate validation, a bundled root store, Aura's
  own random source, and a check for an implausible system clock.
- Memory and disk cache with validation.
- Cookies with Secure, HttpOnly, SameSite, name prefixes and the public
  suffix list.</p>
<a href="/page/44.html" title="Next">More</a></div>
<h2 class="section" id="s45">Security and privacy</h2>
<div class="para"><p>- HSTS with a stored list and a bundled preload list; mixed content
  blocking; HTTPS-first mode.
- Same-origin policy, CORS, Content-Security-Policy, `nosniff`,
  Referrer-Policy.
- Do Not Track and Global Privacy Control; third-party cookie blocking;
  tracker and advertisement blocking from a host list.
- Script limits: memory cap, stack cap, an interrupt for runaway scripts.
- Downloads are never opened or run automatically.
- Security indicator, certificate viewer, certificate error page.
- Every parser that reads network data is bounds-checked, run under
  sanitizers on the host, and fuzzed. Mac OS 9 has no memory protection,
  so this is the largest exposure.</p>
<a href="/page/46.html" title="Next">More</a></div>
<h2 class="section" id="s47">Application</h2>
<div class="para"><p>- Windows and tabs (top row or left column), navigation bar, address
  field with completion and search, status of hovered links, loading
  progress, Stop.
- Bookmarks with a manager and import and export; history with a window;
  downloads with a manager; cookies with a manager; preferences with all
  panes.
- Find in page, text selection and copy, contextual menus (link, image,
  selection), Save Page As, view source, text zoom, local files, dragging
  a link or file onto the window.
- Session restore; recovery from out-of-memory per page.
- Multiprocessing Services: page work runs off the main task and the
  interface stays responsive.
- Internet Config: registration as the default browser, the `GURL` Apple
  event, helper applications for other schemes.</p>
<a href="/page/48.html" title="Next">More</a></div>
<h2 class="section" id="s49">Not in version 1</h2>
<div class="para"><p>- A JavaScript JIT, and Gmail-class web applications at usable speed. A
  bytecode cache and lazy compilation go in if they are cheap.
- Audio and video, WebRTC, WebGL, WebAssembly.
- Web Workers, service workers, IndexedDB.
- WebSockets and canvas 2D. These are the first two to pull in if an
  acceptance site needs them.
- CSS animations and transitions beyond jumping to the end state, 3D
  transforms, filters, blend modes, container queries.
- Right-to-left and vertical text, complex-script shaping (Arabic,
  Indic).
- HTTP/2, HTTP/3, TLS client certificates.
- Printing, private windows, saved passwords in the Keychain, extensions,
  sync.
- A Carbon build for Mac OS X; 68k Macs.
# Aura</p>
<a href="/page/50.html" title="Next">More</a></div>
<div class="para"><p>A web browser for Mac OS 9 on PowerPC, with its engine written from
scratch in C: HTML parser, CSS, layout, rasterizer and networking. It uses
two third-party libraries, QuickJS-ng for JavaScript and BearSSL for TLS.</p>
<a href="/page/51.html" title="Next">More</a></div>
<div class="para"><p>Aura is in early development and does not browse the web yet.</p>
<a href="/page/52.html" title="Next">More</a></div>
<h2 class="section" id="s53">Build</h2>
<div class="para"><p>To build and test on the development machine, then build the Mac OS 9
application, run:</p>
<a href="/page/54.html" title="Next">More</a></div>
<div class="para"><p>```bash
./build.sh
```</p>
<a href="/page/55.html" title="Next">More</a></div>
<div class="para"><p>The Mac OS 9 build needs Retro68's PowerPC toolchain. Set `RETRO68` to its
folder, or keep `os9-protect` next to this repository.</p>
